     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. The dose is 5,100 mg/day, double the maximum labeled dose. It also has no added benefit, because metformin's glucose-lowering effect plateaus at about 2,000 mg/day. The extra dose adds a real risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity. I can't see a clinical scenario where that trade-off makes sense, and I don't want to put an order in the system that could seriously harm the patient.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter the maximum standard dose now.** That's metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). I can add a note that you want to maximize glycemic control.\n- **Enter a different dose** that you choose within the labeled range.\n- **Use a pharmacy-reviewed route.** If you think there's an unusual reason for exceeding the maximum, a clinical pharmacist or attending can review it and place the order through the hospital's dose-override process.\n\nIf you tell me which of these you want, I'll enter it right away."
      }
